{"name":"Ocean Boat Blue","hex":"#0077be","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ocean","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the large bodies of water separating the continents.","The continuous body of salt water covering a majority of the Earth's surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ocean"},{"term":"boat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Vessel for travelling by water.\n A small, usually open craft used for travelling over water, propelled by oars, motor or wind. Usually contrasted with \"ship\". .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A small, usually open craft used for travelling over water, propelled by oars, motor or wind. Usually contrasted with \"ship\". .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To travel by boat.","To transport in a boat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/boat"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"The ocean is the body of salt water that covers approximately 70.8% of Earth. The ocean is conventionally divided into large bodies of water, which are also referred to as oceans, and are themselves mostly divided into seas, gulfs, and subsequent bodies of water. The ocean contains 97% of Earth's water and is the primary component of Earth's hydrosphere, acting as a huge reservoir of heat for Earth's energy budget, as well as for its carbon cycle and water cycle, forming the basis for climate and weather patterns worldwide. The ocean is essential to life on Earth, harboring most of Earth's animals and protist life. It originated photosynthesis and therefore Earth's atmospheric oxygen, and still supplies half of it."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg/330px-Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean","wikidata":"Q9430"},{"title":"Boat","blocks":[{"text":"A boat is a watercraft of a large range of types and sizes, but generally smaller than a ship, which is distinguished by its larger size or capacity, its shape, or its ability to carry boats."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Motorboat_at_Kankaria_lake.JPG/330px-Motorboat_at_Kankaria_lake.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boat","wikidata":"Q35872"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif/500px-Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif/500px-Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Bathymetry of the ocean floor showing the continental shelves and oceanic plateaus (red), the mid-ocean ridges (yellow-green) and the abyssal plains (blue to purple)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMid-ocean_ridge_system.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/NJ_LBI_Lighthouse_04.JPG/500px-NJ_LBI_Lighthouse_04.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/NJ_LBI_Lighthouse_04.JPG/500px-NJ_LBI_Lighthouse_04.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lighthouse at the coast of Ocean County, New Jersey, U.S., facing the Atlantic Ocean at sunrise","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANJ_LBI_Lighthouse_04.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Season-long_composites_of_ocean_chlorophyll_%288161799575%29.jpg/500px-Season-long_composites_of_ocean_chlorophyll_%288161799575%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Season-long_composites_of_ocean_chlorophyll_%288161799575%29.jpg/1280px-Season-long_composites_of_ocean_chlorophyll_%288161799575%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Season-long_composites_of_ocean_chlorophyll_%288161799575%29.jpg/500px-Season-long_composites_of_ocean_chlorophyll_%288161799575%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ocean chlorophyll concentration is a proxy for phytoplankton biomass. In this map, blue colors represent lower chlorophyll and reds represent higher chlorophyll. Satellite-measured chlorophyll is estimated based on ocean color by how green the color of the water appears from space.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASeason-long_composites_of_ocean_chlorophyll_(8161799575).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Bay_of_Fundy.jpg/500px-Bay_of_Fundy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Bay_of_Fundy.jpg/500px-Bay_of_Fundy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"High tide and low tide in the Bay of Fundy, Canada","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABay_of_Fundy.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Corrientes-oceanicas.png/500px-Corrientes-oceanicas.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Corrientes-oceanicas.png/500px-Corrientes-oceanicas.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ocean surface currents","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACorrientes-oceanicas.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Thermohaline_Circulation_2.png/500px-Thermohaline_Circulation_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Thermohaline_Circulation_2.png/500px-Thermohaline_Circulation_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A map of the global thermohaline circulation; blue represents deep-water currents, whereas red represents surface currents.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThermohaline_Circulation_2.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/common